> It certainly conflicts with the "You destroy the stone golem!" message.

I've often thought that the `kill' versus `destroy' distinction in
NetHack was a bit odd. It took me a while just to work out what the
distinction actually was (it turns out you can only `kill' things
which are `alive', which lets out the various undead, golems etc).

For me, `destroy' carries the implication of something not being
there any more, whereas `kill' means it's still there but is lying
motionless on the floor rather than pursuing its own agenda.
Therefore, it would seem more natural to me for the `kill'/`destroy'
distinction to hinge on whether the monster left a corpse or statue,
or whether it was so badly pulverised that it effectively ceased to
exist.

(One might argue that in SLASH'EM, at least, the application of
`kill' to undead is currently inconsistent: zombies are carefully
`destroyed' rather than `killed', but you can play an Undead
_Slayer_.)
